e</p><p>(ii) Glucose&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mover><mo>&#8594;</mo><mrow><mi>Ni</mi><mo>/</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mover><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi><munderover><mo>&#8594;</mo><mrow><msub><mfenced><mrow><msub><mi>CH</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mi>CO</mi></mrow></mfenced><mn>2</mn></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">y</mi><mtext>&#8201;&#8201;</mtext><mi>eq</mi><mo>.</mo><mtext>&#8201;&#8201;</mtext><mi>of</mi></mrow></munderover></math>&nbsp;acetyl derivative</p><p>(iii) Glucose&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><munderover><mo>&#8594;</mo><mrow><msub><mfenced><mrow><msub><mi>CH</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mi>CO</mi></mrow></mfenced><mn>2</mn></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi><mtext>&#8201;</mtext><mi>eq</mi><mo>.</mo><mtext>&#8201;</mtext><mi>of</mi></mrow></munderover></math>&nbsp;acetyl derivative &#39;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">x</mi></math>&#39; , &#39;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">y</mi></math>&#39; and &#39;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></math>&#39; in these reactions are respectively.</p>","options":["<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn></math>&nbsp;&amp;&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>6</mn></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>6</mn></math>&nbsp;&amp;&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>5</mn></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>5</mn></math>&nbsp;&amp;&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>5</mn></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>6</mn></math>&nbsp;&amp;&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>5</mn></math>"],"answer":1,"answers":[1],"questionType":"singleCorrect","subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Biomolecules","exam":"JEE Main","source":"Quizrr PYQ Chapterwise","solution":"","_bank":"quizrr_pyq_chapter","_testId":"69de21fb7e39d99b57bc5baa","_marksId":"648b5a67417cc3fb48d66981"},{"id":"qz_69de4f922ee0e063d92a0a01","_quizrrId":"69de4f922ee0e063d92a0a01","q":"Glucose and Galactose are having identical configuration in all the positions except position.","options":["<math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>3</mn></math>","<math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>4</mn></math>","<math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>5</mn></math>","<math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn></math>"],"answer":1,"answers":[1],"questionType":"singleCorrect","subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Biomolecules","exam":"JEE Main","source":"Quizrr PYQ Chapterwise","solution":"The structures of Glucose and Galactose are:<br  /><img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fdiagrams%2Fqx-book-ee7903de83e63689.png?alt=media&v=stem2\" style=\"width: 160px; height: 250px;\" / data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-ee7903de83e63689.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-ee7903de83e63689.png\"><img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fdiagrams%2Fqx-book-8e47b0cf2882fbe1.png?alt=media&v=stem2\" style=\"width: 160px; height: 239px;\" / data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-8e47b0cf2882fbe1.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-8e47b0cf2882fbe1.png\"><br  />Hence, Glucose and Galactose differ only in the arrangement at <math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>4</mn></math> carbon","_bank":"quizrr_pyq_chapter","_testId":"69de21fb7e39d99b57bc5baa"},{"id":"qz_69de4f922ee0e063d92a0a06","_quizrrId":"69de4f922ee0e063d92a0a06","q":"Identify correct conversion during acidic hydrolysis from the following :\n(A) starch gives galactose.\n(B) cane sugar gives equal amount of glucose and fructose.\n(C) milk sugar gives glucose and galactose.\n(D) amylopectin gives glucose and fructose.\n(E) amylose gives only glucose.\n\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below :","options":["(A), (B) and (C) only","(B), (C) and (E) only","(C), (D) and (E) only","(B), (C) and (D) only"],"answer":1,"answers":[1],"questionType":"singleCorrect","subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Biomolecules","exam":"JEE Main","source":"Quizrr PYQ Chapterwise","solution":"$(\\mathrm{A})$ Starch $\\xrightarrow{\\mathrm{H}^{+} / \\mathrm{H}_2 \\mathrm{O}}$ Glucose\n(B) Cane sugar $\\xrightarrow{\\mathrm{H}^{+} / \\mathrm{H}_2 \\mathrm{O}}$ glucose + fructose (Sucrose) $50 \\% \\quad 50 \\%$\n(C) Milk sugar $\\xrightarrow{\\mathrm{H}^{+} / \\mathrm{H}_2 \\mathrm{O}}$ glucose + galactose (Lactose)\n(D) Amylopectin $\\xrightarrow{\\mathrm{H}^{+} / \\mathrm{H}_2 \\mathrm{O}}$ Glucose\n(E) Amylose $\\xrightarrow{\\mathrm{H}^{+} / \\mathrm{H}_2 \\mathrm{O}}$ Glucose\nSo, correct options are B,C,and E only.","_bank":"quizrr_pyq_chapter","_testId":"69de21fb7e39d99b57bc5baa"},{"id":"qz_69de4f922ee0e063d92a0a0b","_quizrrId":"69de4f922ee0e063d92a0a0b","q":"Which one of the following is correct structure for cytosine ?","options":["<img src=\"https://cdn-question-pool..app/pyq/jee_main/b3c022f7-d389-4e82-945a-f5224f5c6c1b-image.png\"><br>","<img src=\"https://cdn-question-pool..app/pyq/jee_main/6cff6b25-e7fe-4a53-ac95-307958e6a2ec-image.png\"><br>","<img src=\"https://cdn-question-pool..app/pyq/jee_main/f8f7cad5-c726-4f9a-aa89-2f919d3a60d1-image.png\"><br>","<img src=\"https://cdn-question-pool..app/pyq/jee_main/cb650ffd-dce6-4dc6-8768-aab6279db0ff-image.png\"><br>"],"answer":2,"answers":[2],"questionType":"singleCorrect","subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Biomolecules","exam":"JEE Main","source":"Quizrr PYQ Chapterwise","solution":"<p>The correct structure of cytosine</p><p><img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fdiagrams%2Fqx-book-323e2d1f6a32ad0b.png?alt=media&v=stem2\" style=\"width: 135px; height: 182px;\" / data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-323e2d1f6a32ad0b.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-323e2d1f6a32ad0b.png\"></p>","_bank":"quizrr_pyq_chapter","_testId":"69de21fb7e39d99b57bc5baa","_resolvedFrom":"marks_login_id","_marksId":"648b5a6b417cc3fb48d6737d"},{"id":"qz_69de4f922ee0e063d92a0a10","_quizrrId":"69de4f922ee0e063d92a0a10","q":"Which structure of protein remains intact after coagulation of egg white on boiling?","options":["Primary","Tertiary","Secondary","Quaternary"],"answer":0,"answers":[0],"questionType":"singleCorrect","subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Biomolecules","exam":"JEE Main","source":"Quizrr PYQ Chapterwise","solution":"<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">When an egg is boiled, the soluble globular protein, albumin present in it is converted to insoluble fibrous protein. </p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">During denaturation, its biological activity is lost. Boiling an egg causes denaturation of its protein resulting in loss of its quaternary, tertiary and secondary structures.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">But the primary structure (representing the sequence of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">α</mi></math> amino acids) remains intact.</p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Hence option A is the answer.</p>","_bank":"quizrr_pyq_chapter","_testId":"69de21fb7e39d99b57bc5baa"},{"id":"qz_69de4f922ee0e063d92a0a15","_quizrrId":"69de4f922ee0e063d92a0a15","q":"Which one of the following is a water soluble vitamin, that is not excreted easily?","options":["&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Vitamin</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Vitamin</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Vitamin</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi><mn>6</mn></msub></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Vitamin</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi><mn>12</mn></msub></math>"],"answer":3,"answers":[3],"questionType":"singleCorrect","subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Biomolecules","exam":"JEE Main","source":"Quizrr PYQ Chapterwise","solution":"B group vitamins and vitamin C are soluble in water except vitamin $\\mathrm{B}_{12}$ all are excreted easily.","_bank":"quizrr_pyq_chapter","_testId":"69de21fb7e39d99b57bc5baa"},{"id":"qz_69de4f922ee0e063d92a0a1a","_quizrrId":"69de4f922ee0e063d92a0a1a","q":"Which of the following statements is correct?","options":["Gluconic acid can form cyclic (acetal/hemiacetal) structure","Gluconic acid is a dicarboxylic acid","Gluconic acid is a partial oxidation product of glucose","Gluconic acid is obtained by oxidation of glucose with <math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub></math>"],"answer":1,"answers":[1],"questionType":"singleCorrect","subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Biomolecules","exam":"JEE Main","source":"Quizrr PYQ Chapterwise","solution":"","_bank":"quizrr_pyq_chapter","_testId":"69de21fb7e39d99b57bc5baa"},{"id":"qz_69de4f922ee0e063d92a0a1f","_quizrrId":"69de4f922ee0e063d92a0a1f","q":"<p><img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fdiagrams%2Fqx-book-693bc78d4817d2e3.png?alt=media&v=stem2\" style=\"width: 203px; height: 215px;\" / data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-693bc78d4817d2e3.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-693bc78d4817d2e3.png\"></p><p>The compound <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#39;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi><mo>&#39;</mo></math> is a complementary base of .......... in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>DNA</mi></math> strands.</p>","options":["Uracil","Guanine","Adenine","Cytosine"],"answer":2,"answers":[2],"questionType":"singleCorrect","subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Biomolecules","exam":"JEE Main","source":"Quizrr PYQ Chapterwise","solution":"Given structure is Thymine and Thymine being paired with adenine","_bank":"quizrr_pyq_chapter","_testId":"69de21fb7e39d99b57bc5baa"},{"id":"qz_69de4f922ee0e063d92a0a24","_quizrrId":"69de4f922ee0e063d92a0a24","q":"<p>Match items of Row I with those of Row II.</p><p><strong>Row I:</strong></p><p>(P)<br /><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://cdn-question-pool..app/pyq/jee_main/580cf0d6-5037-4f03-8396-c91e3c455599-9894443_1.PNG\" style=\"width: 190px; height: 171px;\" / data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-c6822a98d5c50350.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-c6822a98d5c50350.png\"></p><p>(Q)<br /><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://cdn-question-pool..app/pyq/jee_main/d9efd3dd-2580-40a6-b593-47df2355a4d6-9894443_2.PNG\" style=\"width: 186px; height: 162px;\" / data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-7d6835ec5b771cec.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-7d6835ec5b771cec.png\"></p><p>(R)<br /><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://cdn-question-pool..app/pyq/jee_main/c0d9c87e-5420-4fe2-914b-4de94b2ee860-9894443_3.PNG\" style=\"width: 236px; height: 137px;\" / data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-5af7cbb2cbc0ba01.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-5af7cbb2cbc0ba01.png\"></p><p>(S)<br /><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://cdn-question-pool..app/pyq/jee_main/a24ee8ae-9309-4fb9-8b0b-f53fc9cb70e1-9894443_4.PNG\" style=\"width: 242px; height: 134px;\" / data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-e471a8ae0b3c989c.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/a